</figure>
<figure id="S4.F8" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.25401v1/kernel_o_performance_33k.svg" id="S4.F8.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:548/137;" width="548" height="137"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 8: </span>Normalized inference performance at different <math id="S4.F8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{N}</annotation></semantics></math> for sparse GEMM-<math id="S4.F8.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>O</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O</annotation></semantics></math>, where token length is 17K.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S4.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We perform ablation experiments (Table <a href="#S4.T3" title="Table 3 ‣ 4.4 Ablation study ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ FlashOmni: A Unified Sparse Attention Engine for Diffusion Transformers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>) on Flux to assess how the interval <math id="S4.SS4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{N}</annotation></semantics></math> and caching order <math id="S4.SS4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}</annotation></semantics></math> affect generation quality. First<span id="S4.SS4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text">-</span>order caching (<math id="S4.SS4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}=1</annotation></semantics></math>) delivers the largest performance gains, while higher orders degrade quality, highlighting the limits of simulation in capturing real<span id="S4.SS4.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text">-</span>world behavior. Despite its bias, first<span id="S4.SS4.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text">-</span>order caching remains more faithful than direct reuse. Increasing <math id="S4.SS4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{N}</annotation></semantics></math> offers greater acceleration but at the cost of noticeable quality loss.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS4.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We further conduct ablation experiments (Figure <a href="#S4.F8" title="Figure 8 ‣ 4.4 Ablation study ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ FlashOmni: A Unified Sparse Attention Engine for Diffusion Transformers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>) to evaluate the speedup of GEMM-<math id="S4.SS4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>O</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O</annotation></semantics></math> across settings <math id="S4.SS4.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{N}</annotation></semantics></math> with a sequence length of 17K. As <math id="S4.SS4.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{N}</annotation></semantics></math> increases, the overall speedup consistently improves but falls short of the theoretical speedup predicted by reduced FLOPs. For example, when <math id="S4.SS4.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{N}=4,6,8" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>6</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{N}=4,6,8</annotation></semantics></math>, the measured 93.1%, 87.7%, and 84.7% of theoretical speedup , respectively. This discrepancy arises because GEMM-<math id="S4.SS4.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>O</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O</annotation></semantics></math> requires multiple decoding operations along the reduction axis on CUDA cores; even at 90% sparsity, while GEMM-<math id="S4.SS4.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q</annotation></semantics></math> alone can achieve a near<span id="S4.SS4.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text">-</span>linear <math id="S4.SS4.p2.m7" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="9\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>9</mn><mo lspace="0.222em">×</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">9\times</annotation></semantics></math> speedup, GEMM-<math id="S4.SS4.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>O</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O</annotation></semantics></math> only reaches <math id="S4.SS4.p2.m9" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="6.39\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>6.39</mn><mo lspace="0.222em">×</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">6.39\times</annotation></semantics></math> due to these additional overheads. As <math id="S4.SS4.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{N}</annotation></semantics></math> grows, this effect becomes increasingly pronounced in the aggregated GEMM-<math id="S4.SS4.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>O</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O</annotation></semantics></math> speedup.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5 </span>Conclusion</h2>

<div id="S5.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We propose FlashOmni, a unified sparse attention engine that is applicable to any Diffusion Transformers. FlashOmni abstracts an <span id="S5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Update–Dispatch</span> paradigm with three key designs: it unifies multi-granularity spare strategies with flexible sparse symbols, supports efficient arbitrary sparse attention through a general kernel, and optimizes sparse GEMMs in attention linear layers to remove redundant computation. Experiments demonstrate that FlashOmni achieves significant end-to-end acceleration across diverse models without retraining, while preserving generation quality.</p>
</div>
</section>
